  "editorial": {
    "approach": "The brute force is the direct prefix DP: dp[g][i] is the smallest achievable largest-piece sum when the first i numbers are cut into g pieces, and dp[g][i] = min over j < i of max(dp[g-1][j], prefix[i] - prefix[j]). Prefix sums make each piece sum O(1), and the table is rolled down to two rows of length n+1. The optimal turns the objective around and binary searches the answer itself. For a candidate limit L, feasibility is monotone (a limit that works keeps working as it grows) and testable in one greedy pass: start with one piece, add numbers while the running sum stays within L, otherwise start a new piece and count it; the limit is feasible when the final piece count is at most m. Searching the smallest feasible L between max(nums) and sum(nums) returns the answer.",
    "why_optimal": "The candidate range is [max(nums), sum(nums)], and with nums[i] <= 10000 and n <= 100 its width is at most 10^6, so about 20 halvings are enough; each check is a single O(n) pass, giving O(n log S) with S = sum(nums), and no memory beyond a few counters. The DP also runs in a fraction of a second at these bounds, but its cost grows with the number of pieces (500k table entries when m = n = 100), while the binary search never even reads m during the search. The greedy feasibility test is exactly right because filling each piece as full as possible leaves the maximum room for the pieces that follow, an exchange argument: any cut that leaves a piece with spare capacity can shift its last element to the next piece without increasing the largest sum.",
    "pitfalls": "The greedy pass must start with groups = 1 rather than 0 so a single piece covering everything is allowed (that is the m = 1 case, whose answer is sum(nums)). The binary search's low bound must be max(nums), not 0: with a low bound of 0 the feasibility test would report success on limits no single element can satisfy. Take the midpoint with integer division and use the `lo < hi` / `hi = mid` invariant, otherwise the loop can spin forever when hi = lo + 1. Prefix sums reach 10^6 here, so a port to a language with 32-bit overflow semantics must widen the accumulator. Finally, m = nums.length must fall out of the same code path and give max(nums)."
  },
